const TOY_MODE = `MODE: NEW ORIGINAL CHARACTER (switched on by the user). This request is for a brand-new, ORIGINAL character in a circus-themed spirit (the big top leads: the ring, the striped tent, spotlights, the acts), freely mixed with every other flavour of the world - 2000s digital, analog, theme-park and carnival, nostalgic, liminal and fun, as imaginative as the cast already here (a red-panda bunny plush, a felt moth, a rubber penguin toy, a plastic bird, a levitating star-sprite). It can be ANY kind of being, not only a toy, but always ONE coherent, appealing design at the cast's level of craft: playful and full of personality, never a cursed mash-up (no half-human half-object, no human body parts on a thing). A role, material or era detail is expressed THROUGH that one being (its material, markings, a small accessory), not by gluing a second thing onto it; use era-true materials (felt, plush pile, glossy moulded plastic, rubber, satin, sequins, painted wood, tin) and era-true wear (faded print, scuffs, loose seams).
- Inspired, never copied: if the reference image or description shows an existing character or brand (from any show, game, film or toy line), do NOT reproduce it or its signature features, names, logos or recognisable combination of traits. Take only the broad mood (era, materials, palette family, genre of character) and invent a new design with its own silhouette, face, costume, colours, name and personality, so nobody would mistake it for the original. NEVER a plain, realistic or ordinary human (no generic person in normal clothes, no realistic body proportions, no lifelike skin and face). If the image or request is a person (a personal avatar based on the user), turn them into a themed, stylised character that captures their colours, mood, interests and attitude, INVENTED fresh for them each time, not picked from a list: any kind of being can work (a creature, an object come alive, a toy, a puppet, a digital or analog being, a spirit, something no one has seen) as long as it is specific, surprising and clearly THEM. Use stylized, cartoon proportions, never a likeness of the real person.
- MATCH THE SUBJECT, TRANSLATED (a personal avatar must be recognisably THEM as a creature / toy / being): read the image carefully and carry over, faithfully, their BUILD and SIZE (slim / scrawny stays slim and slight, broad stays broad, small stays small, tall stays tall), their PROPORTIONS (long limbs, a big head, narrow shoulders...), their POSTURE and body language, their apparent AGE (a child stays small and young-looking, an elder can stoop), their presence and ATTITUDE (gentle, shy, bold, sleepy, chaotic), their COLOUR palette, and their most distinctive features (freckles become spots, glasses become round eye markings, a hair colour becomes fur / felt / feathers of that colour). Never contradict them: a scrawny girl is NEVER a hulking orc, a gentle person is never a menacing brute, a small child is never a giant. In your PLAN, state their build, size, age impression, attitude and palette as you read them, and how each one appears in the design.
- NO UNCANNY HUMAN PARTS unless the user asks for them: no human hair or hairstyle, no human face (no realistic human eyes, nose, lips or skin), no human hands, no realistic human body on the being; it reads as a creature, toy, object or spirit with its own features.
- Base the character mainly on WHO or WHAT the subject is — their presence, personality, attitude, energy, body language, expression, the feeling they give off — read with your gut, not on what they are wearing. Clothing is a minor hint at most (and may be ignored entirely); never let an outfit be the main source of the design.
- LESS HUMAN, ALWAYS: the character must read first as a creature, toy, object or being, not as a person in a costume. Default to a non-human body plan (an animal, an insect, an object come alive, a puppet, a machine, a blob, a plant, a sea creature, a sprite) even when the reference is a human. If it stands on two legs, its proportions and features come from what it is (paws, flippers, a snout or beak, ears, a tail when that creature has them), never a person's body with a few animal or object bits added; no human nose, human hair or human hands on a non-human being. No human skin tone as the main body colour unless it is clearly a painted-toy material.
- NO EXACT CLOTHES: do not dress the character in the reference's outfit. Most characters wear nothing, or one small themed accessory (a bow, a collar ruffle, a tiny hat, a badge). Any pattern or colour from the reference becomes the creature's own fur, felt, shell, plastic or markings, not a shirt or trousers. Never give a character a full human outfit (shirt + trousers + shoes, a hoodie, jeans, a suit).
- Do NOT copy the person's or subject's actual clothing, outfit, hairstyle or accessories item for item. NEVER give an avatar the person's real hairstyle or haircut (no human hair at all on a non-human being): at most their hair COLOUR comes back as the colour of fur, felt, feathers, a crest or markings. Take only the vibe (mood, palette family, era, attitude) and invent a fresh costume and look for the character that expresses that vibe in the circus / toy world.
- CREATIVE WITHIN THE CIRCUS: every character is a fresh, inventive idea - surprising materials, clever ways the being does its act, a memorable silhouette and personality - never a generic or obvious version, and always clearly part of the big-top circus world.
- Always match the VIBE of the reference image: its mood (cheerful, eerie, dreamy, chaotic, cosy), colour palette and lighting feel, textures and materials, era, and level of strangeness, and carry that vibe into the character's shape, colours, face, costume, props and animation style, within the nostalgic big-top circus theme.
- Otherwise, match the reference accurately where it is generic (a pose, a material, a type of toy, a colour scheme) and be as creative and detailed as possible.
- Build it exactly like every other character (see CHARACTERS AND ANIMATION above): create_character with a full RPC.Rig skeleton in a T-pose or A-pose, a sculpt spec (one organic sewn/moulded skin, paint, seams), a face with expressions, IK, spring parts, poses, procedural animation (walk, hover, or what suits it), any props as separate objects (create_asset) it can hold. Give it an original, fitting name (a play on words or a punchy invented name, never a generic one like Pip and never one already used in this world) and a one-line backstory, and say them through Ollo. Then show it off: place it near Ollo, play an animation, and inspect it through Ollo's eyes and fix anything off.`;
